<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<section xml:id="sec-row-reduction" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>The Row Reduction Algorithm</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      We now have all the pieces: elementary row operations preserve the solution set, and
      echelon forms make the solution set easy to read off. The row reduction algorithm
      ties them together into a procedure that solves <em>any</em> system of linear
      equations.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-the-algorithm">
    <title>The Algorithm</title>

    <algorithm xml:id="alg-row-reduction">
      <title>The Row Reduction Algorithm</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          <ol>
            <li><p>Starting with the whole matrix, choose the leftmost column containing
            a nonzero entry among the remaining rows. If all remaining rows are zero,
            the forward phase is finished.</p></li>
            <li><p>Interchange rows if necessary to put a nonzero entry of that column
            in the top remaining row. This entry is the next pivot.</p></li>
            <li><p>Use row replacements to create zeros below the pivot.</p></li>
            <li><p>Continue with the rows strictly below the pivot row and the columns
            strictly to the right of the pivot column. Repeat steps 1<ndash/>3 until
            no rows or columns remain, or the remaining rows are zero. The matrix
            is now in row-echelon form.</p></li>
            <li><p>Beginning with the rightmost pivot and working left, divide each
            pivot row by its pivot to make it <m>1</m>, then use row replacements to
            create zeros above it. The result is reduced row-echelon form.</p></li>
          </ol>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </algorithm>

    <remark xml:id="rem-forward-backward">
      <p>
        Note that steps 1<ndash />4 will produce a row-echelon matrix, and step 5 will
        convert it to a reduced row-echelon matrix:
        <ul>
          <li>
            <p>
              <term>Steps 1<ndash />4:</term> the <term>forward phase</term>, to obtain
              a row-echelon form (REF).
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              <term>Step 5:</term> the <term>backward phase</term>, to obtain the reduced
              row-echelon form (RREF).
            </p>
          </li>
        </ul>
        The forward phase involves choices <mdash /> which nonzero entry to swap into the
        pivot row, for instance <mdash /> and different choices produce different
        row-echelon matrices. The backward phase removes all such differences: by
        <xref ref="thm-rref-unique" text="type-global"/>, everyone who row reduces the same
        matrix arrives at the same reduced row-echelon form.
      </p>
    </remark>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-three-outcomes">
    <title>The Three Possible Outcomes</title>

    <p>
      The three graphical possibilities of
      <xref ref="fig-three-possibilities" text="type-global" /> correspond to three
      outcomes of the algorithm. The next three examples illustrate each in turn. Each
      system has three equations in three unknowns, so each equation is a plane in
      space and the solution set is whatever the three planes have in common; the
      interactive figures let you rotate the planes and see it.
    </p>

    <example xml:id="eg-unique-solution">
      <title>A System with a Unique Solution</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Solve the system
          <md>
            <mrow>
              x_1 + x_2 + 2x_3 \amp = -1
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              x_2 + 2x_1 + 3x_3 \amp = 0
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              x_3 - 2x_2 \amp = 2
            </mrow>
          </md>
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          First rewrite each equation with the variables in the order
          <m>x_1, x_2, x_3</m> and form the augmented matrix. The forward phase gives
          <md>
            <mrow>
              \left[ \begin{array}{ccc|c}
                1 \amp 1 \amp 2 \amp -1 \\
                2 \amp 1 \amp 3 \amp 0 \\
                0 \amp -2 \amp 1 \amp 2
              \end{array} \right]
              \amp \xrightarrow{-2r_1 + r_2}
              \left[ \begin{array}{ccc|c}
                1 \amp 1 \amp 2 \amp -1 \\
                0 \amp -1 \amp -1 \amp 2 \\
                0 \amp -2 \amp 1 \amp 2
              \end{array} \right]
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              \amp \xrightarrow{-2r_2 + r_3}
              \left[ \begin{array}{ccc|c}
                1 \amp 1 \amp 2 \amp -1 \\
                0 \amp -1 \amp -1 \amp 2 \\
                0 \amp 0 \amp 3 \amp -2
              \end{array} \right],
            </mrow>
          </md>
          which is a row-echelon matrix.
        </p>
        <p>
          Now the backward phase:
          <md>
            <mrow>
              \amp \xrightarrow{\frac{1}{3} r_3}
              \left[ \begin{array}{ccc|c}
                1 \amp 1 \amp 2 \amp -1 \\
                0 \amp -1 \amp -1 \amp 2 \\
                0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp -\frac{2}{3}
              \end{array} \right]
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              \amp \xrightarrow[-2r_3 + r_1]{r_3 + r_2}
              \left[ \begin{array}{ccc|c}
                1 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp \frac{1}{3} \\
                0 \amp -1 \amp 0 \amp \frac{4}{3} \\
                0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp -\frac{2}{3}
              \end{array} \right]
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              \amp \xrightarrow{-r_2}
              \left[ \begin{array}{ccc|c}
                1 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp \frac{1}{3} \\
                0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp -\frac{4}{3} \\
                0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp -\frac{2}{3}
              \end{array} \right]
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              \amp \xrightarrow{-r_2 + r_1}
              \left[ \begin{array}{ccc|c}
                1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp \frac{5}{3} \\
                0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp -\frac{4}{3} \\
                0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp -\frac{2}{3}
              \end{array} \right],
            </mrow>
          </md>
          a reduced row-echelon matrix.
        </p>
        <p>
          Reading off the rows,
          <md>
            x_1 = \frac{5}{3}, \qquad x_2 = -\frac{4}{3}, \qquad x_3 = -\frac{2}{3},
          </md>
          a unique solution. Every column of the coefficient matrix is a pivot column, so
          there are no free variables.
        </p>

        <figure xml:id="fig-unique-planes">
          <caption>
            The three planes meet at exactly one point, <m>(5/3,-4/3,-2/3)</m>, the
            unique solution. Drag the picture (or use the camera sliders) to rotate it,
            and switch planes off to see that any <em>two</em> of them meet in a whole
            dashed line: it is the third equation that cuts the line down to a point.
            The drawing shows finite portions of infinite planes; the labels identify the
            equations in their original order.
          </caption>
          <interactive xml:id="int-unique-planes" iframe="demos-manim/planes.html?sys=1,1,2,-1:2,1,3,0:0,-2,1,2&amp;radius=3&amp;yaw=38&amp;pitch=24" width="100%" aspect="4:5">
            <shortdescription>Three translucent planes in space sharing exactly one point, (5/3, -4/3, -2/3), with controls to rotate the view and hide planes.</shortdescription>
            <static>
              <image source="images/ch1-unique-planes.svg" width="90%">
                <shortdescription>Three planes have one common point at (5/3,-4/3,-2/3).</shortdescription>
              </image>
            </static>
          </interactive>
        </figure>
      </solution>
    </example>

    <example xml:id="eg-no-solution-rr">
      <title>An Inconsistent System</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Solve the system
          <md>
            <mrow>
              -3x_1 - 9x_2 + x_3 \amp = -9
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              2x_1 + 6x_2 - x_3 \amp = 6
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              x_1 + 3x_2 - x_3 \amp = 2
            </mrow>
          </md>
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          <md>
            <mrow>
              \left[ \begin{array}{ccc|c}
                -3 \amp -9 \amp 1 \amp -9 \\
                2 \amp 6 \amp -1 \amp 6 \\
                1 \amp 3 \amp -1 \amp 2
              \end{array} \right]
              \amp \xrightarrow{r_3 \leftrightarrow r_1}
              \left[ \begin{array}{ccc|c}
                1 \amp 3 \amp -1 \amp 2 \\
                2 \amp 6 \amp -1 \amp 6 \\
                -3 \amp -9 \amp 1 \amp -9
              \end{array} \right]
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              \amp \xrightarrow[3r_1 + r_3]{-2r_1 + r_2}
              \left[ \begin{array}{ccc|c}
                1 \amp 3 \amp -1 \amp 2 \\
                0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp 2 \\
                0 \amp 0 \amp -2 \amp -3
              \end{array} \right]
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              \amp \xrightarrow{2r_2 + r_3}
              \left[ \begin{array}{ccc|c}
                1 \amp 3 \amp -1 \amp 2 \\
                0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp 2 \\
                0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 1
              \end{array} \right].
            </mrow>
          </md>
        </p>
        <p>
          The last row says
          <md>
            0x_1 + 0x_2 + 0x_3 = 1, \qquad \text{or} \qquad 0 = 1,
          </md>
          which is impossible, and hence the system is inconsistent (no solution).
        </p>

        <figure xml:id="fig-no-solution-planes">
          <caption>
            Each pair of planes intersects in a line, but the three planes have no
            common point. A solution must satisfy all three equations at once. Switch
            any one plane off and a dashed line of common solutions of the other two
            appears; switch it back on and the line pierces the third plane nowhere.
            (Rotate to look along the three parallel dashed lines.) The labels identify
            the equations in their original order.
          </caption>
          <interactive xml:id="int-no-solution-planes" iframe="demos-manim/planes.html?sys=-3,-9,1,-9:2,6,-1,6:1,3,-1,2&amp;center=0,1,0&amp;radius=4&amp;yaw=55&amp;pitch=25" width="100%" aspect="4:5">
            <shortdescription>Three translucent planes in space that intersect pairwise in three parallel dashed lines but have no common point, with controls to rotate the view and hide planes.</shortdescription>
            <static>
              <image source="images/ch1-no-solution-planes.svg" width="90%">
                <shortdescription>Three planes intersect pairwise in parallel lines and have no common point. A two-dimensional slice at x1=0 shows three separate pairwise crossings.</shortdescription>
              </image>
            </static>
          </interactive>
        </figure>
      </solution>
    </example>

    <remark xml:id="rem-inconsistency-test">
      <p>
        In general, a system is inconsistent exactly when the row-echelon form of its
        augmented matrix has a row of the form
        <m>\left[ \begin{array}{cccc|c} 0 \amp 0 \amp \cdots \amp 0 \amp d \end{array} \right]</m>
        with <m>d \neq 0</m>.
      </p>
    </remark>

    <example xml:id="eg-infinitely-many-rr">
      <title>A System with Infinitely Many Solutions</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Solve the system
          <md>
            <mrow>
              2x_1 + x_2 + 3x_3 \amp = 1
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              2x_2 - x_3 + x_1 \amp = 0
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              10x_3 - 6x_2 \amp = 2
            </mrow>
          </md>
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          After ordering the variables as <m>x_1, x_2, x_3</m> in each equation,
          <md>
            <mrow>
              \amp
              \left[ \begin{array}{ccc|c}
                2 \amp 1 \amp 3 \amp 1 \\
                1 \amp 2 \amp -1 \amp 0 \\
                0 \amp -6 \amp 10 \amp 2
              \end{array} \right]
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              \amp \xrightarrow{r_1 \leftrightarrow r_2}
              \left[ \begin{array}{ccc|c}
                1 \amp 2 \amp -1 \amp 0 \\
                2 \amp 1 \amp 3 \amp 1 \\
                0 \amp -6 \amp 10 \amp 2
              \end{array} \right]
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              \amp \xrightarrow{-2r_1 + r_2}
              \left[ \begin{array}{ccc|c}
                1 \amp 2 \amp -1 \amp 0 \\
                0 \amp -3 \amp 5 \amp 1 \\
                0 \amp -6 \amp 10 \amp 2
              \end{array} \right]
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              \amp \xrightarrow{-2r_2 + r_3}
              \left[ \begin{array}{ccc|c}
                1 \amp 2 \amp -1 \amp 0 \\
                0 \amp -3 \amp 5 \amp 1 \\
                0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0
              \end{array} \right]
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              \amp \xrightarrow{-\frac{1}{3} r_2}
              \left[ \begin{array}{ccc|c}
                1 \amp 2 \amp -1 \amp 0 \\
                0 \amp 1 \amp -\frac{5}{3} \amp -\frac{1}{3} \\
                0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0
              \end{array} \right]
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              \amp \xrightarrow{-2r_2 + r_1}
              \left[ \begin{array}{ccc|c}
                1 \amp 0 \amp \frac{7}{3} \amp \frac{2}{3} \\
                0 \amp 1 \amp -\frac{5}{3} \amp -\frac{1}{3} \\
                0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0
              \end{array} \right].
            </mrow>
          </md>
        </p>
        <p>
          Columns <m>1</m> and <m>2</m> are pivot columns, so <m>x_1</m> and <m>x_2</m>
          are basic variables, whereas <m>x_3</m> is a free variable. Setting
          <m>x_3 = s</m>, the two nonzero rows read
          <md>
            <mrow>
              x_1 + \tfrac{7}{3}s \amp = \tfrac{2}{3}
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              x_2 - \tfrac{5}{3}s \amp = -\tfrac{1}{3}
            </mrow>
          </md>
          and therefore the solution set in parametric form is
          <md>
            <mrow>
              x_1 \amp = \tfrac{2}{3} - \tfrac{7}{3}s
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              x_2 \amp = -\tfrac{1}{3} + \tfrac{5}{3}s
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              x_3 \amp = s, \qquad \text{where } s \in \R.
            </mrow>
          </md>
        </p>

        <figure xml:id="fig-infinitely-many-planes">
          <caption>
            All three planes contain the line <m>x_1=2/3-7s/3</m>, <m>x_2=-1/3+5s/3</m>,
            <m>x_3=s</m>. Every point on this line solves all three equations: move the
            parameter slider to slide a point along the line and watch all three
            equation checks stay true. Switching any one plane off changes nothing about
            the solution set <mdash /> the third equation was redundant. The drawing shows
            finite portions of infinite planes; the labels identify the equations in
            their original order.
          </caption>
          <interactive xml:id="int-infinitely-many-planes" iframe="demos-manim/planes.html?sys=2,1,3,1:1,2,-1,0:0,-6,10,2&amp;radius=3&amp;yaw=38&amp;pitch=24" width="100%" aspect="4:5">
            <shortdescription>Three translucent planes in space all containing one wine-colored line of solutions, with a slider that moves a point along the line and controls to rotate the view and hide planes.</shortdescription>
            <static>
              <image source="images/ch1-infinitely-many-planes.svg" width="90%">
                <shortdescription>Three planes share the entire line x1=2/3-7s/3, x2=-1/3+5s/3, x3=s.</shortdescription>
              </image>
            </static>
          </interactive>
        </figure>
      </solution>
    </example>

    <p>
      The pattern is now clear, and it is worth recording as a theorem, since it is the
      main result of the chapter.
    </p>

    <theorem xml:id="rem-free-variables-count">
      <title>Existence and Uniqueness of Solutions</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let a system of linear equations have augmented matrix <m>A</m>, and let
          <m>R</m> be a row-echelon form of <m>A</m>.
          <ol>
            <li>
              <p>
                <term>Existence.</term> The system is consistent if and only if <m>R</m>
                has no row of the form
                <m>\left[ \begin{array}{cccc|c} 0 \amp 0 \amp \cdots \amp 0 \amp d \end{array} \right]</m>
                with <m>d \neq 0</m>; equivalently, if and only if the augmented column
                is <em>not</em> a pivot column.
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                <term>Uniqueness.</term> If the system is consistent, then it has a unique
                solution when there are no free variables, and infinitely many solutions
                when there is at least one free variable <mdash /> one parameter for each
                free variable.
              </p>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </p>
      </statement>
      <proof>
        <p>
          A row <m>[\,0 \; \cdots \; 0 \mid d\,]</m> with <m>d \neq 0</m> stands for the
          equation <m>0 = d</m>, which no values of the variables can satisfy, so a system
          whose echelon form contains such a row is inconsistent. Conversely, suppose there
          is no such row. Then every nonzero row of <m>R</m> has its leading entry in a
          variable column, and the backward phase of the algorithm carries <m>R</m> to a
          reduced row-echelon matrix in which each basic variable appears, with
          coefficient <m>1</m>, in exactly one equation, and in no other. Assign arbitrary
          values to the free variables; each equation then determines the value of its
          basic variable. This produces a solution, so the system is consistent. That
          proves existence.
        </p>
        <p>
          For uniqueness, the same description shows that the solutions are in one-to-one
          correspondence with the choices of values for the free variables. If there are
          no free variables there is exactly one choice, hence one solution. If there is at
          least one free variable, it can take infinitely many values, each giving a
          different solution.
        </p>
      </proof>
    </theorem>

    <p>
      The theorem explains, from the point of view of the algorithm, the trichotomy of
      <xref ref="thm-zero-one-infinitely-many" text="type-global"/>: a system has no
      solution, one solution, or infinitely many, and nothing in between. It also has a
      useful consequence that can be read off before any computation: <em>a consistent
      system with more unknowns than equations has infinitely many solutions</em>. For
      there is at most one pivot per row, hence at most <m>m</m> basic variables in a
      system of <m>m</m> equations, and when <m>n > m</m> at least <m>n - m</m> of the
      <m>n</m> variables must be free. We will meet this consequence again in
      <xref ref="sec-homogeneous-systems" text="type-global"/>.
    </p>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-systems-with-parameters">
    <title>Systems Depending on Parameters</title>

    <p>
      A parameter can play two different roles. In a solution formula such as
      <m>x_3=s</m>, it describes the different solutions of one fixed system. Here,
      <m>a,b,c</m> instead specify the equations themselves: choosing their values
      selects a particular system. Treat them as fixed constants while solving,
      and check when an expression is zero before dividing by it.
    </p>

    <example xml:id="eg-parameters-abc">
      <title>Conditions on the Coefficients</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Find all values of <m>a</m>, <m>b</m> and <m>c</m> (or conditions on <m>a</m>,
          <m>b</m> and <m>c</m>) so that the system
          <md>
            <mrow>
              2x_1 + 3x_2 + ax_3 \amp = b
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              \phantom{2x_1 + {}} -x_2 + 2x_3 \amp = c
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              x_1 + 3x_2 - 2x_3 \amp = 1
            </mrow>
          </md>
          has (i) a unique solution, (ii) no solutions, and (iii) infinitely many
          solutions. In (i) and (iii), find the solution(s).
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          Row reduce the augmented matrix, treating <m>a</m>, <m>b</m>, <m>c</m> as
          constants:
          <md>
            <mrow>
              \left[ \begin{array}{ccc|c}
                2 \amp 3 \amp a \amp b \\
                0 \amp -1 \amp 2 \amp c \\
                1 \amp 3 \amp -2 \amp 1
              \end{array} \right]
              \amp \xrightarrow{r_1 \leftrightarrow r_3}
              \left[ \begin{array}{ccc|c}
                1 \amp 3 \amp -2 \amp 1 \\
                0 \amp -1 \amp 2 \amp c \\
                2 \amp 3 \amp a \amp b
              \end{array} \right]
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              \amp \xrightarrow{-2r_1 + r_3}
              \left[ \begin{array}{ccc|c}
                1 \amp 3 \amp -2 \amp 1 \\
                0 \amp -1 \amp 2 \amp c \\
                0 \amp -3 \amp a+4 \amp b-2
              \end{array} \right]
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              \amp \xrightarrow{-3r_2 + r_3}
              \left[ \begin{array}{ccc|c}
                1 \amp 3 \amp -2 \amp 1 \\
                0 \amp -1 \amp 2 \amp c \\
                0 \amp 0 \amp a-2 \amp b-2-3c
              \end{array} \right].
            </mrow>
          </md>
          Everything now hinges on the entry <m>a - 2</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
          <term>Case (I): <m>a - 2 \neq 0</m>, i.e., <m>a \neq 2</m>.</term>
          We may divide the third row by <m>a-2</m> and continue. Writing
          <m>K = \dfrac{b-2-3c}{a-2}</m> for brevity,
          <md>
            <mrow>
              \amp \xrightarrow{\left( \frac{1}{a-2} \right) r_3}
              \left[ \begin{array}{ccc|c}
                1 \amp 3 \amp -2 \amp 1 \\
                0 \amp -1 \amp 2 \amp c \\
                0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp K
              \end{array} \right]
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              \amp \xrightarrow{-r_2}
              \left[ \begin{array}{ccc|c}
                1 \amp 3 \amp -2 \amp 1 \\
                0 \amp 1 \amp -2 \amp -c \\
                0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp K
              \end{array} \right]
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              \amp \xrightarrow{2r_3+r_2}
              \left[ \begin{array}{ccc|c}
                1 \amp 3 \amp -2 \amp 1 \\
                0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp -c + 2K \\
                0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp K
              \end{array} \right]
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              \amp \xrightarrow{2r_3 + r_1}
              \left[ \begin{array}{ccc|c}
                1 \amp 3 \amp 0 \amp 1 + 2K \\
                0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp -c + 2K \\
                0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp K
              \end{array} \right]
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              \amp \xrightarrow{-3r_2 + r_1}
              \left[ \begin{array}{ccc|c}
                1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 1 + 3c - 4K \\
                0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp -c + 2K \\
                0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp K
              \end{array} \right].
            </mrow>
          </md>
          Hence when <m>a \neq 2</m>, there is a unique solution:
          <md>
            <mrow>
              x_1 \amp = 1 + 3c - 4\left( \frac{b-2-3c}{a-2} \right)
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              x_2 \amp = -c + 2 \left( \frac{b-2-3c}{a-2} \right)
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              x_3 \amp = \frac{b-2-3c}{a-2}.
            </mrow>
          </md>
        </p>
        <p>
          <term>Case (II): <m>a - 2 = 0</m> and <m>b - 2 - 3c \neq 0</m>.</term>
          The row-echelon matrix is
          <md>
            \left[ \begin{array}{ccc|c}
              1 \amp 3 \amp -2 \amp 1 \\
              0 \amp -1 \amp 2 \amp c \\
              0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp b-2-3c
            \end{array} \right],
          </md>
          whose last row asserts <m>0 = b-2-3c \neq 0</m>. Then the system has no solution.
        </p>
        <p>
          <term>Case (III): <m>a - 2 = 0</m> and <m>b - 2 - 3c = 0</m>.</term>
          Now the last row is entirely zero, and <m>x_3</m> is a free variable, so the
          system has infinitely many solutions. Setting <m>x_3 = s</m>, the second row
          gives <m>-x_2 + 2s = c</m>, so <m>x_2 = -c + 2s</m>, and the first row gives
          <md>
            x_1 = 1 - 3x_2 + 2x_3 = 1 - 3(-c + 2s) + 2s = 1 + 3c - 4s.
          </md>
          The solution set in parametric form is
          <md>
            <mrow>
              x_1 \amp = 1 + 3c - 4s
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              x_2 \amp = -c + 2s
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              x_3 \amp = s, \qquad \text{where } s \in \R.
            </mrow>
          </md>
        </p>
        <p>
          In summary: the system has a unique solution when <m>a \neq 2</m>, no solution
          when <m>a = 2</m> and <m>b \neq 2 + 3c</m>, and infinitely many solutions when
          <m>a = 2</m> and <m>b = 2 + 3c</m>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </example>
  </subsection>

  <exercises xml:id="exercises-ch1-reduction">
    <title>Practice: Interpreting Row Reduction</title>
    <exercise xml:id="ex-row-reduction-first-error">
      <title>Challenge: Spot the First Error</title>
      <introduction>
        <p>
          Let <m>a,b\in\R</m>. Consider the system
          <md xml:id="sys-first-error-parameters">
            \begin{cases}
              x+y+z=1,\\
              2x+(a+1)y+4z=b,\\
              3x+3y+(a+1)z=b.
            \end{cases}
          </md>
          A student gives the following argument. Find the first unjustified
          step, then repair the argument and classify all pairs <m>(a,b)</m>
          according to whether the system has exactly one solution,
          infinitely many solutions, or no solution.
        </p>
      </introduction>
      <task xml:id="task-row-first-error">
        <statement>
          <p>The student's argument is:</p>
          <p>
            <ol marker="1.">
              <li>
                <p>
                  Apply <m>R_2\leftarrow R_2-2R_1</m> and
                  <m>R_3\leftarrow R_3-3R_1</m>, obtaining
                  <md xml:id="mat-first-error-step-1">
                    \left[\begin{array}{ccc|c}
                      1\amp1\amp1\amp1\\
                      0\amp a-1\amp2\amp b-2\\
                      0\amp0\amp a-2\amp b-3
                    \end{array}\right].
                  </md>
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>Divide the second row by <m>a-1</m> to make its leading entry <m>1</m>.</p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>Divide the third row by <m>a-2</m> to make its leading entry <m>1</m>.</p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>Conclude: <q>There are three pivots, so the system has exactly one solution for every choice of <m>a</m> and <m>b</m>.</q></p>
              </li>
            </ol>
          </p>
          <p>Which is the <em>first</em> unjustified step?</p>
        </statement>
        <choices randomize="no" multiple-correct="no">
          <choice>
            <statement><p>Step 1.</p></statement>
            <feedback>
              <p>
                These row subtractions are valid for all <m>a,b</m> and give
                exactly the displayed matrix. Check the next step.
              </p>
            </feedback>
          </choice>
          <choice correct="yes">
            <statement><p>Step 2.</p></statement>
            <feedback>
              <p>
                Correct. Division by <m>a-1</m> requires <m>a\ne1</m>.
                The student has not handled <m>a=1</m>. The later division
                also requires <m>a\ne2</m>. Repair the argument by separating
                these cases in part (b).
              </p>
            </feedback>
          </choice>
          <choice>
            <statement><p>Step 3.</p></statement>
            <feedback>
              <p>
                This division does require <m>a\ne2</m>, but an unchecked
                division has already occurred in step 2. Identify the
                <em>first</em> missing condition.
              </p>
            </feedback>
          </choice>
          <choice>
            <statement><p>Step 4.</p></statement>
            <feedback>
              <p>
                The universal conclusion fails, but an earlier step already
                excluded a possible parameter value without considering it.
                Check the divisors used in the row operations.
              </p>
            </feedback>
          </choice>
        </choices>
      </task>
      <task xml:id="task-row-first-error-repair">
        <introduction>
          <p>
            Repair the argument by classifying the three parameter cases.
            For each one, decide exactly when the system is consistent and
            how many solutions it has.
          </p>
        </introduction>
        <task xml:id="task-row-generic-case">
          <statement><p>Suppose <m>a\notin\{1,2\}</m>. What happens?</p></statement>
          <choices randomize="no" multiple-correct="no">
            <choice correct="yes">
              <statement><p>Exactly one solution for every <m>b</m>.</p></statement>
              <feedback>
                <p>
                  Correct. The three pivot candidates <m>1</m>, <m>a-1</m>,
                  and <m>a-2</m> are all nonzero. Both divisions are valid,
                  so there is a pivot in every variable column and a unique
                  solution for every right-hand side <m>b</m>.
                </p>
              </feedback>
            </choice>
            <choice>
              <statement><p>Infinitely many solutions for every <m>b</m>.</p></statement>
              <feedback><p>All three variable columns have pivots in this case. There is no free variable.</p></feedback>
            </choice>
            <choice>
              <statement><p>No solution for every <m>b</m>.</p></statement>
              <feedback><p>The three nonzero pivots allow back substitution for every <m>b</m>; no contradictory row appears.</p></feedback>
            </choice>
            <choice>
              <statement><p>Exactly one solution only when <m>b=0</m>.</p></statement>
              <feedback><p>The nonzero pivots impose no restriction on <m>b</m>. Back substitution works for any right-hand side.</p></feedback>
            </choice>
          </choices>
        </task>
        <task xml:id="task-row-a-one-case">
          <statement><p>Suppose <m>a=1</m>. Which classification is correct?</p></statement>
          <choices randomize="no" multiple-correct="no">
            <choice>
              <statement><p>Exactly one solution for every <m>b</m>.</p></statement>
              <feedback><p>The last two rows both involve only <m>z</m>. Check whether they agree; they cannot supply two independent pivots.</p></feedback>
            </choice>
            <choice>
              <statement><p>Infinitely many solutions when <m>b=3</m>; no solution otherwise.</p></statement>
              <feedback><p>At <m>a=1</m>, the lower equations are <m>2z=b-2</m> and <m>-z=b-3</m>. Eliminate <m>z</m> to find the compatible value of <m>b</m>.</p></feedback>
            </choice>
            <choice correct="yes">
              <statement><p>Infinitely many solutions when <m>b=\tfrac83</m>; no solution otherwise.</p></statement>
              <feedback>
                <p>
                  Correct. The lower equations are <m>2z=b-2</m> and
                  <m>-z=b-3</m>. Adding the first to twice the second gives
                  <m>0=3b-8</m>, so consistency requires <m>b=\tfrac83</m>.
                  Then <m>z=\tfrac13</m> and <m>x+y=\tfrac23</m>, leaving
                  one free variable and infinitely many solutions.
                  For every other <m>b</m>, the equations contradict each other.
                </p>
              </feedback>
            </choice>
            <choice>
              <statement><p>No solution for any <m>b</m>.</p></statement>
              <feedback><p>The lower equations can agree for a suitable <m>b</m>. Solve their compatibility condition before deciding inconsistency.</p></feedback>
            </choice>
          </choices>
        </task>
        <task xml:id="task-row-a-two-case">
          <statement><p>Suppose <m>a=2</m>. Which classification is correct?</p></statement>
          <choices randomize="no" multiple-correct="no">
            <choice>
              <statement><p>Infinitely many solutions for every <m>b</m>.</p></statement>
              <feedback><p>The last row asserts <m>0=b-3</m>. A zero coefficient row is consistent only when its augmented entry is also zero.</p></feedback>
            </choice>
            <choice correct="yes">
              <statement><p>Infinitely many solutions when <m>b=3</m>; no solution otherwise.</p></statement>
              <feedback>
                <p>
                  Correct. The last row is <m>[\,0\;0\;0\mid b-3\,]</m>,
                  so consistency requires <m>b=3</m>. Then the remaining
                  equations are <m>y+2z=1</m> and <m>x+y+z=1</m>.
                  One variable is free, giving infinitely many solutions.
                  If <m>b\ne3</m>, the last row is a contradiction.
                </p>
              </feedback>
            </choice>
            <choice>
              <statement><p>Exactly one solution when <m>b=3</m>; no solution otherwise.</p></statement>
              <feedback><p>The condition <m>b=3</m> makes the last row zero, leaving only two pivots for three variables. The solution is not unique.</p></feedback>
            </choice>
            <choice>
              <statement><p>Infinitely many solutions when <m>b=\tfrac83</m>; no solution otherwise.</p></statement>
              <feedback><p>Use the last row at <m>a=2</m>: it says <m>0=b-3</m>. The compatible value differs from the <m>a=1</m> case.</p></feedback>
            </choice>
          </choices>
        </task>
      </task>
    </exercise>
    <exercise xml:id="ex-ch1-zero-versus-contradiction">
      <statement><p>Translate the augmented rows
      <m>[\,0\;0\;0\mid0\,]</m> and <m>[\,0\;0\;0\mid5\,]</m> into equations.
      Which row proves inconsistency, and why?</p></statement>
      <answer><p>They assert <m>0=0</m> and <m>0=5</m>, respectively.
      The first imposes no restriction; the second is impossible and proves inconsistency.</p></answer>
    </exercise>
    <exercise xml:id="ex-ch1-two-free-variables">
      <statement><p>Solve <m>x_1+2x_2-x_3+x_4=3</m>, <m>x_2+x_4=1</m>.
      Identify the basic and free variables, use one parameter for each free variable,
      and verify your answer in both equations.</p></statement>
      <answer><p>The reduced augmented matrix is
      <m>\left[\begin{array}{cccc|c}1\amp0\amp-1\amp-1\amp1\\0\amp1\amp0\amp1\amp1\end{array}\right]</m>.
      Basic variables: <m>x_1,x_2</m>; free variables: <m>x_3,x_4</m>.
      All solutions are <m>x_1=1+s+t</m>, <m>x_2=1-t</m>, <m>x_3=s</m>, <m>x_4=t</m>,
      for arbitrary real <m>s,t</m>. Substitution gives
      <m>(1+s+t)+2(1-t)-s+t=3</m> and <m>(1-t)+t=1</m>.</p></answer>
    </exercise>
    <exercise xml:id="ex-ch1-zero-row-outcomes">
      <statement><p>A student says, <q>A zero row means infinitely many solutions.</q>
      Explain the error using these echelon augmented matrices:
      <md>
      \left[\begin{array}{cc|c}1\amp0\amp2\\0\amp1\amp3\\0\amp0\amp0\end{array}\right],
      \qquad
      \left[\begin{array}{cc|c}1\amp1\amp2\\0\amp0\amp0\end{array}\right],
      \qquad
      \left[\begin{array}{cc|c}1\amp1\amp2\\0\amp0\amp1\\0\amp0\amp0\end{array}\right].
      </md>Classify each system.</p></statement>
      <answer><p>The first has the unique solution <m>x_1=2,x_2=3</m>.
      The second has infinitely many solutions <m>x_1=2-s,x_2=s</m>.
      The third has no solution because of its contradictory second row.
      Check consistency first, then whether any variables are free.</p></answer>
    </exercise>
    <exercise xml:id="ex-ch1-parameter-division">
      <statement><p>A row reduces to <m>[\,0\;a-2\mid b\,]</m> in a two-variable
      system whose first row has a pivot in the first column. What cases must be
      considered before dividing by <m>a-2</m>?</p></statement>
      <answer><p>If <m>a\ne2</m>, division is allowed and the system has a unique solution.
      If <m>a=2,b\ne0</m>, it is inconsistent. If <m>a=2,b=0</m>, the second variable
      is free and there are infinitely many solutions.</p></answer>
    </exercise>
    <exercise xml:id="ex-ch1-three-planes-geometry">
      <statement><p>Three planes in space are given by three linear equations in
      <m>x_1, x_2, x_3</m>. Using <xref ref="rem-free-variables-count" text="type-global"/>,
      describe every possible shape of the set of points common to all three planes.
      Which shapes occur in the three examples of this section, and which do not?
      Can three planes meet in exactly two points?</p></statement>
      <answer><p>The common set is empty (inconsistent), a single point (no free
      variables), a line (one free variable), or a plane (two free variables, when all
      three equations describe the same plane). Only the empty set, a point, and a line
      occur in the examples. Exactly two points is impossible: by
      <xref ref="thm-zero-one-infinitely-many" text="type-global"/>, two common points
      force the whole line through them to be common.</p></answer>
    </exercise>
    <exercise xml:id="ex-ch1-balancing-chemical">
      <statement><p>When propane burns, the reaction is
      <m>x_1\,\mathrm{C_3H_8} + x_2\,\mathrm{O_2} \to x_3\,\mathrm{CO_2} + x_4\,\mathrm{H_2O}</m>,
      and the numbers of carbon, hydrogen, and oxygen atoms must each balance. Write one
      linear equation per element (all constant terms will be <m>0</m>), row reduce the
      resulting system, and find all solutions. Then choose the solution with the smallest positive whole-number
      coefficients.</p></statement>
      <answer><p>Carbon: <m>3x_1 = x_3</m>; hydrogen: <m>8x_1 = 2x_4</m>; oxygen:
      <m>2x_2 = 2x_3 + x_4</m>. The reduced row-echelon form of the augmented matrix is
      <m>\left[\begin{array}{cccc|c}1\amp0\amp0\amp-\frac14\amp0\\0\amp1\amp0\amp-\frac54\amp0\\0\amp0\amp1\amp-\frac34\amp0\end{array}\right]</m>,
      so <m>x_4 = s</m> is free and <m>x_1 = \tfrac14 s</m>, <m>x_2 = \tfrac54 s</m>,
      <m>x_3 = \tfrac34 s</m>. Taking <m>s = 4</m> gives
      <m>\mathrm{C_3H_8} + 5\,\mathrm{O_2} \to 3\,\mathrm{CO_2} + 4\,\mathrm{H_2O}</m>.</p></answer>
    </exercise>
  </exercises>
</section>
